<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abu Ishaq al-Sahili</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1290–1346</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abu Ishaq al-Sahili was an Andalusi poet from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> who accompanied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa-musa">Mansa Musa</a> back from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a> in the 1320s. Tradition credits him with designing buildings for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa">mansa</a>, including a royal audience chamber and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djinguereber-mosque">Djinguereber Mosque</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a>. He settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> and died at Timbuktu in 1346.</p></div>
